      "events": "Thursday farewell late-night meal, and Lu Chuan receives a friend request from Xu Ning, who has not contacted him for three years. Meng Yao explains that the farewell photo in the old classmates group was the source of the news. Xu Ning uses the return of a book with a hand-drawn map as a reason to meet him on Friday at 08:30. Among the moving boxes Lu Chuan deletes and rewrites his reply again and again and finally agrees clearly; she says that she is not here to delay his departure.",
      "state": "The two restore direct contact and agree to return the book the next day, and neither yet knows the life of the other during these three years.",
      "handoff": "The next episode begins with the book-return meeting at the bookshop door on Friday.",
      "text": "Scene 1 Thursday 22:10 EXT. Haiting · riverside night food stalls (three days before leaving the city)\nThe spatula knocks against the rim of the iron wok. Lu Chuan splits the disposable chopsticks apart and lines up the two of unequal length. His colleague Meng Yao raises a cup and wishes him a smooth start in Nanzhou. Someone at the table asks whether the new office is far from the sea, and he says that he does not know, the rental contract is already signed, he starts on Monday, and the train is at 18:40 on Sunday.\nMeng Yao: You have arranged it so cleanly, like dragging one folder into another.\nLu Chuan: Best to clear the cache as well.\nEveryone laughs. The phone buzzes by the bowl, and on the screen appears a name he has not seen for three years: Xu Ning. The note on the friend request is a single line: “I heard that you are leaving. Would it be convenient to add me back?”\nThe chopsticks of Lu Chuan stop in mid air. The last time he saw a message from her was on that stormy night three years ago. She asked where the key was, he answered the guard room, and they had never been in touch since.\nMeng Yao pushes the grilled aubergine toward him and notices that the smile on his face has not come down with it.\nMeng Yao: I posted the farewell photo in the old classmates group, you do not mind? I only said that you were changing jobs.\nLu Chuan: No. Is she in that group too?\nMeng Yao nods and does not lean over to look at his screen. Lu Chuan darkens the phone and drinks a mouthful of tea that has already gone cold.\n\nScene 2 Thursday 22:35 EXT. The sidewalk beside the night food stalls\nHis colleagues are still arguing about who pays, and Lu Chuan says that he is taking a call and walks to a tree the street lamp does not reach. He has no call at all. He opens the request page and looks for a long time at the blank space next to Accept.\nXu Ning has changed her picture to a window, and on the sill there is nothing he recognizes. He had imagined that at a reunion he would be calm, that he would ask how she had been, and now he does not even know whether to use an exclamation mark.\nHe presses accept and types: “Leaving on Sunday. Something you need?” Seeing the question mark, he deletes the second half and writes again: “Leaving on Sunday. How have you been lately?”\nXu Ning replies quickly: “Not bad. I have one of your books here, I only found it while tidying the shelf. Do you want to take it back?”\nLu Chuan: Which book?\nXu Ning: The one you drew a riverside map on the title page of. The corners of the pages were wrinkled by coffee.\nHe remembers. It is a collection of essays about ordinary people walking, and after he bought it he only ever read seriously the annotations she wrote in the blank spaces. When they rented a place together they argued for ten minutes about which side the desk lamp should go on, yet never once discussed what to do about leaving this city.\nLu Chuan types “keep it, it is yours”, and does not send it. He then types “I can post it”, and deletes that too.\nLu Chuan: Are you free tomorrow morning?\nXu Ning: Half past eight, outside Old Pages Bookshop. I have to hand in a translation at half past nine, so I can only stay for a while.\nLu Chuan: All right.\n\nScene 3 Thursday 23:05 INT. The rented flat of Lu Chuan\nWhen the door opens, a carton that was not sealed properly tips over at his feet. Lu Chuan steadies the box and coils up the charging cable that fell out. Only a mattress, a folding chair and two cups are left in the room, and the landlord is coming at noon on Sunday to check the flat.\nMeng Yao sends a message: “Home yet? Tomorrow you still have to hand over the computer at the office, do not be late.” He replies with one word, home, and then taps into the chat with Xu Ning. The chat history from three years ago broke together with the old phone, and the page is as blank as two people meeting for the first time.\nHe sets the alarm for seven, and finds that an alarm for seven already exists, set for the moving company, with a date on Sunday. He creates a new one, writes “pick up the book”, and then deletes the note.\nOutside the window the last bus turns the corner. Lu Chuan sits beside the boxes and thinks of the thing he did not ask her: if it really was just to return a book, why did it have to happen before he leaves?\nThe screen suddenly lights up. Xu Ning adds a sentence: “You do not have to clear a whole day on purpose. I am not here to delay your departure.”\nThe fingers of Lu Chuan hover for a moment, and he replies: “I know. See you tomorrow.” This time he does not delete it."
