      "events": "Xu Ning returns the book at the door of the Old Page bookshop, with a note inside saying that the old shop has closed. Shen An asks Lu Chuan to help repair the charity sale table on Saturday. At breakfast Lu Chuan discovers that her tastes have changed, and further that she had long since come back to west-of-the-river Haiting after only half a year in Luo Harbor; Xu Ning admits plainly that returning the book was only her reason for seeing him. He asks her to have lunch after she hands in her draft, and accepts the arrangement even if she may not make it in time.",
      "state": "The two no longer treat old impressions as the present situation, and Xu Ning states clearly that she wants to see him.",
      "handoff": "In the next episode the noon deadline for the draft slips, testing whether they can state specific times clearly.",
      "text": "Scene 1 Friday 08:25 EXT. Outside the Old Pages Bookshop (two days before leaving the city)\nLu Chuan arrives five minutes early. Old Pages is not open yet, and a card for the Saturday secondhand book charity sale hangs on the door handle. Xu Ning comes from the corner of the street with no coffee in her hand, only a plain paper bag. She stops two steps away and says good morning, and only then does he notice that he has zipped his jacket right to the top.\nXu Ning: You still arrive early.\nLu Chuan: You are not late either.\nThe sentence leaves them both quiet for a moment, as if they had touched an old wound by accident. Xu Ning hands the book over. The paper bag has been changed, but the corners of the pages are still curled. Lu Chuan turns to the title page, where next to the small restaurant on the map she has added a line: “It has closed, do not waste the trip.” The colour of the ink is very new.\nLu Chuan: Have you been there recently?\nXu Ning: Last month. The landlord rented the shop to a flower store.\nLu Chuan nods. He lived in Haiting for seven years, and some changes he only hears about when he is about to leave.\n\nScene 2 Friday 08:40 EXT. The breakfast shop beside the bookshop\nShen An, the owner of the bookshop, comes out of the alley, and on seeing them only says that the wind is strong at the door and that they should go and eat something first. He and Xu Ning are running the charity sale on Saturday, and the money from the books will add reading tables for the nearby community; they are short of exactly one person who can repair a table leg.\nShen An: Lu Chuan, you used to do that kind of thing. Forget it if you have no time, do not let it delay your move.\nLu Chuan says that he still has to hand over the computer in the afternoon, and that he can come for an hour on Saturday morning. Xu Ning looks at him as if she wants to remind him of something, and in the end only nods.\nThey sit down by the window in the breakfast shop. The owner asks how many bowls, and Lu Chuan says out of habit “two bowls, no scallion in one”, then lifts his head. Xu Ning smiles: “I eat scallion now.” He changes the order, with a trace of embarrassment on his face.\nXu Ning: The job in Nanzhou, do you like it a lot?\nLu Chuan: Public transport interfaces. Not what I know best, but I want to try.\nXu Ning: That is good.\nShe says it very seriously, without the persuading he had expected. Instead he does not know how to go on. She looks down, finishes reading a client message, and tells him that she now works as a freelance translator, handing in drafts in the morning and checking book lists with the community in the afternoon.\nLu Chuan: You did not stay in Luogang?\nXu Ning: That exchange ended after half a year. After I came back I lived in Hexi.\nHe holds the spoon tight. He had somehow always believed that after she left three years ago she had never come back.\nLu Chuan: I thought you had stayed away all this time.\nXu Ning: No. After coming back I did think about looking for you, but I did not know which sentence to start with.\nShe glances at the time, puts the phone away, and does not explain that sentence any further.\n\nScene 3 Friday 09:10 EXT. The intersection\nXu Ning is going to a shared office. Lu Chuan holds the book and waits with her at the light, and the red number counts down from forty. He wants to ask why she did not get in touch after coming back, and feels that the question would land on himself just the same.\nLu Chuan: I am not leaving Haiting because of you.\nXu Ning looks at him: I did not think that.\nLu Chuan: I was only afraid that you would misunderstand.\nXu Ning: And I did not suddenly start doing badly because I heard that you were leaving.\nShe is not angry, and instead laughs softly. Lu Chuan finally lets his shoulders relax. Both of them are blocking a judgement for the other that does not exist.\nThe light turns green, Xu Ning takes one step forward and then turns back.\nXu Ning: When I said that I would return the book, it really was not only about the book. I wanted to see you. Yesterday I did not dare to write that directly.\nThe traffic has not moved yet, and the intersection is very quiet. Lu Chuan hears the soymilk machine in the breakfast shop across the street.\nLu Chuan: I am eating at half past twelve today, at that noodle shop we used to go to. If you have handed in your draft and have not eaten, you could come.\nXu Ning: I may not make it in time. If I do not, I will tell you.\nLu Chuan: All right, do not rush.\nShe crosses the road without promising that she will definitely appear. He does not lower his head to check the time over and over either, but puts the book into his bag and walks toward the office. The old route on the title page is still there, only now, for the first time, it has gained a midday that neither of them yet knows whether they will meet in."
