      "events": "Lu Chuan talks with his parents and admits to ordinary unease about the new job, and that family care no longer rests entirely on him. Xu Ning tells Meng Yao that she was the one who made contact; then she takes the initiative to tell Lu Chuan that the four-month North Shore project is still undecided, rather than notifying him only after the conclusion. On the phone the two make clear what they care about, what frightens them and what is uncertain; they do not force a promise to get back together and agree to fix the table first the next day.",
      "state": "A new long-distance possibility may be disclosed in advance, and both allow the other to remain undecided.",
      "handoff": "In the next episode, on Saturday morning, they test their way of communicating through concrete collaboration.",
      "text": "Scene 1 Friday 18:30 Int. Lu Chuan’s rented flat\nOn the wall by the window a pale square showed where a hanging picture had been taken down. Lu Chuan was working through a checklist: internet cancelled, three keys, movers at 14:00 on Saturday. He put the book she had returned into his carry bag instead of into a cardboard box.\nHis mother called on video, and his father sat beside her slowly peeling a mandarin. Lu Chuan said the new place was four stops from the office, that he would go to Nanzhou first on Sunday, and that he had originally planned to come home to see them next month. His father asked whether he would find a new city hard to get used to, and as usual he almost said “everything is arranged”, then changed it to being afraid he would not find the meeting room on the first day.\nHis father laughed: If you cannot find it, just ask. When you were little you would even ask the bus driver.\nHis mother turned the camera toward the kitchen and lowered her voice a little: This job is the one you want to do, you do not have to prove anything to anyone. We have me and the care worker at home, you do not have to count every minute before calling back.\nLu Chuan saw the pill box beside his father’s hand on the screen. Three years ago he would not even show Xu Ning this much worry, as if being reliable enough gave him the right to ask her to stay.\n\nScene 2 Friday 19:40 Int. Xu Ning’s shared office\nXu Ning finished the last paragraph of the translation and rested her finger on the send key. The client had pushed twice, and she still checked the figures first. Beside the screen was the charity sale rota Shen An had sent for the next morning, with “fix the table, leave before ten” written after the name of Lu Chuan.\nMeng Yao called to ask whether she had got the book back. Xu Ning said it had been returned. Meng Yao paused, and said she had not passed any message for anyone, only that the farewell photo really had made her think of Xu Ning.\nXu Ning: No need to explain. I added him myself.\nMeng Yao: Good then. I was afraid you two would both look at me tomorrow as if I were in charge of the ending.\nXu Ning laughed: Your only job is not to post my work address in the group.\nWhen the call ended she opened the schedule for tomorrow afternoon: 16:00, a remote talk with an out-of-town publisher about a four-month residency translation project. She had passed the first round, the location is North Shore, and she had not decided whether to accept. She wanted to tell Lu Chuan, and also worried that it would sound like a repeat of three years ago. Even now she still pictured a disappointed expression on his behalf before saying anything.\nShe messaged him: “Tomorrow afternoon I have to discuss an out-of-town project, it could mean leaving for four months. I wanted to tell you first, not to ask for your opinion.”\n\nScene 3 Friday 20:15 Int. Lu Chuan’s rented flat / Xu Ning’s office\nLu Chuan’s phone lit up. He was sealing a box, the tape went crooked and left a crease. After reading the message his hands still stopped out of habit, then he put down the tape and pressed voice call directly.\nLu Chuan: Is now a good time?\nXu Ning: I have to lock up in ten minutes, it is fine.\nLu Chuan: I just saw it, and I am not quite sure how to answer. I am afraid I will sound again as if I think you should not go.\nXu Ning: You do not have to get it right first.\nLu Chuan walked over to the small patch of floor without boxes.\nLu Chuan: I do want to see more of you. But that is a different matter from whether you can take the project. Have you already decided you want to go?\nXu Ning: The work is very attractive, and life would have to be rearranged. I have not decided. Before, I thought telling you the result was enough, now I want to say the hesitation out loud too.\nLu Chuan: Then I am listening.\nShe talked about the workload of the residency, the pay and the solitude she feared most, and he did not hurry to reroute his trip through Nanzhou, nor use “we are not back together anyway” to keep himself out of it. When it was his turn he admitted that last night he had almost declined her friend request, because he was afraid she was only being polite, and also afraid she really wanted to start again.\nThe ten minutes were up and half the lights in the corridor went out. Xu Ning shut down the computer and said she had to go.\nLu Chuan: I still have not worked out what starting again means.\nXu Ning: Neither have I. Tomorrow let us fix the table first.\nHe looked down and saw the scrap of waste tape in his hand, and finally laughed out loud. They said goodnight without slipping into any promise. After the call Xu Ning put “residency project” into her own schedule, and Lu Chuan added one item for the next morning: bring the hex key. That was not the answer, but it was something he really could do."
